# Hard rules (root CLAUDE.md)

- **Smart Agent address is the canonical identifier.** Every person, org, service agent, and treasury IS its ERC-4337 SA address. Names, profiles, ERC-8004 entries, ANS handles, HCS topics — all facets pointing AT the canonical address. Cross-package APIs take `Address`, not names. See [ADR-0010](docs/architecture/decisions/0010-smart-agent-canonical-identifier.md) + [spec 220](specs/220-agent-identity-bootstrap.md) for the bootstrap process (deploy → forced-unique name → custody → optional facets).
- **Every Smart Agent is a Person, an Organization, or a Service — nothing else.** PROV-O's trichotomy (`prov:Person`/`prov:Organization`/`prov:SoftwareAgent`), mirrored by `AgentType = 'person' | 'org' | 'service'` in `@agenticprimitives/types`. Treasury, indexer, relay, marketplace, data-source are **Service roles** (profile `serviceType` / app-level kinds), never new classes; UI groups by class and labels by role; app kinds map DOWN to a class, never up into the enum ([ADR-0046](docs/architecture/decisions/0046-agent-classification-person-org-service.md), specs 217/225 §6). **A demo person SA is never also their org SA** — do not reuse “his own SA” as the organization ([agent-rules/demo-person-org-distinct.md](docs/architecture/agent-rules/demo-person-org-distinct.md), `pnpm check:demo-person-org-distinct`).
- **Canonical identity persists; credentials rotate.** Passkeys, SIWE EOAs, hardware wallets are control credential facets — replaceable, not the identity. Credential add / replace / remove is a custody-policy-governed operation (trustee quorum, guardian quorum, multi-credential self-recovery, or multi-sig), NEVER a delegation. The SA address NEVER changes during credential recovery. **Every standing grant is re-approved by the ROTATION ceremony under the new custody epoch, and never by recovery** (spec 410 §1; the pre-epoch sentence "delegations remain valid after rotation" is retired): a rotation is `add → retire → approveDigest×N` in one transaction, a recovery is `add → retire → revokeAll` and the person re-issues from a reviewed list; only the account computes an approved-hash key (`approveDigest`), the registry is never an execute target. See [ADR-0011](docs/architecture/decisions/0011-credential-recovery-and-re-association.md) + [spec 221](specs/221-credential-recovery.md).
- **Specs precede non-trivial code.** New `specs/2XX-*.md` before architecture changes; the spec is the architect-of-record.
- **Always check smart-agent first.** Before designing any non-trivial capability look at the analog in `/home/barb/smart-agent`. New specs MUST include a "Reference: smart-agent patterns to port" section. Deliberate divergence must say why.
- **Package boundaries** are one-directional: `types ← identity-auth ← agent-account ← delegation ← mcp-runtime`, plus `key-custody → delegation`, `tool-policy → mcp-runtime`, and the custody-layer fork `types ← custody` (depended on by `agent-account` and `delegation`; see [spec 213](specs/213-custody-layer-carve-out.md) for the firewall). No back-edges. `tool-policy` and `types` are transport-agnostic (no MCP/A2A/LangChain/Vercel imports).
- **`AgentAccount.sol` is an ERC-7579 modular core — custody governance is a module; sessions + spend are inlined in v1 (ARCH-H4, honest state).** The custody surface (threshold / guardians / recovery / approvals) IS an installed EXECUTOR module (`CustodyPolicy`), and account-governance ops route through it (spec 300). But session acceptance and spend limits are currently INLINED in `AgentAccount.sol` (spend exists only as a delegation-layer caveat, not an account hook), and `MODULE_TYPE_FALLBACK` is unsupported — so the "everything is a module" framing is aspirational for those two. The direction (extract `SessionKeyValidator`, then a spend hook) stands, but do NOT assert sessions/spend are already modules. `specs/209` is the taxonomy target of record; when extracting a module, update it + this rule together. See memory `feedback_erc7579_module_architecture`.
- **HTTPS is required; mTLS is optional; admission is always required.** The normative external profile is A2A over HTTPS. mTLS is *additive transport evidence* on a separate hostname (`mtls.<host>`) — it never establishes owning person, organization, canonical agent, relationship, delegation, purpose, capability, consent, or policy, and never lets a request skip a stage. SPIFFE is an optional workload binding, not identity: which agent a workload may act as is a signed `WorkloadIdentityBinding`, never an inference from a trust-domain path. MCP is a PRIVATE capability interface behind an admitted runtime, absent from every Agent Card (`demo-mcp`'s public `/mcp`+`/mcp/native` are a labelled, deliberately non-conformant interop demo). Optional transports, brokers and workload-identity systems are leaf bindings that depend INWARD; core never depends outward. Ordering that must never invert: `certificate → transport evidence → optional workload binding → application authentication → canonical resolution → Admission` ([ADR-0057](docs/architecture/decisions/0057-a2a-over-https-admission-mtls-optional.md) + [spec 339](specs/339-agentic-primitives-edge-https-admission.md); gate `check:optional-package-boundaries`).
- **DO-local storage is a SERVING-PLANE concern; the vault is the RECORD.** Durable Object storage
  (`state.storage` / `state.storage.sql`) exists for ordering, cursors, receipts, request-id ledgers,
  projections and caches — things that are derived, rebuildable, and needed at request latency. It MUST
  NOT become the home of durable private records, message bodies, grants, or any self-sovereign data.
  Those live in the owner's vault behind demo-mcp, where per-record delegation scope is enforced
  (ADR-0055). **The drift to refuse is DO-local quietly becoming the person/org vault store** — it
  arrives as a performance argument and leaves you with a second enforcement point, a second authority
  model, and records nobody can carry to another Home. The test for any new DO table: *if this DO were
  wiped, is the loss a rebuild or a bereavement?* A rebuild is fine; a bereavement means it belonged in
  the vault. Applies to `InteractionsDO` and to `PrincipalGatewayDO` alike — the latter constructs a
  DO-SQL vault internally, which is why an app MUST inject the MCP-backed one rather than inherit it
  from a constructor ([ADR-0055](docs/architecture/decisions/0055-vaults-are-the-canonical-system-of-record-for-content.md)).
- **Define the capability model ONCE; generate the UX behaviour AND the agent surfaces from it.** Never
  build the UX and then separately teach Ask how the application works. The Ask in Home drives everything
  the Home UX drives because both are projections of one semantic definition: the **ontology** (how the
  domain is shaped) + the **capability** (ADR-0051 id + label — what the agent advertises) + the
  **SKILL.md execution contract** (`SkillExecutionContractV1` — risk, requirement type, `resourceArg`/
  `authorityArg`, approvals, evidence, spec-360 `effects`), joined by the `~/skills` compiler into an
  `AgentHarnessDefinitionV1` and consumed — never authored — here. From that one model come the Ask's tool
  set, the published vocabulary, the A2A card skills, each act's mandate requirement and risk floor, what
  follows the act, and the Home affordance. **If adding a feature means writing an invoker AND a vocabulary
  entry AND a UI handler, the model is not doing its job.** The bugs this is made of: a Treasury playbook
  that promised "every payment leaves a receipt" into a field nothing read, so money moved and neither
  party was told; and `skills:includesSkill` compiled from a hand-kept `CAPABILITY_PROFILE` table instead
  of the contract a domain author wrote. **Behaviour is generated; authority never is** — a generated
  surface is consulted by no verifier (spec 354 §1 / ADR-0041). Hand-adding a `CAPABILITY_PROFILE` row is
  the anti-pattern; the fallback warns per capability so it stays visible.
  ([agent-rules/one-capability-model-generates-both.md](docs/architecture/agent-rules/one-capability-model-generates-both.md), specs 354/355/359)
- **Domain relationships live in the ONTOLOGY, not in prompts, SKILL.md files or app tables.** A claim about how the domain is shaped — "a treasury belongs to the agent that holds it", "an inbox belongs to a person", "an organization pays from its own balance" — goes in `packages/ontology/tbox/*.ttl` with a comment saying what it means and what it is NOT, and code binds to it BY IRI (`packages/ontology/src/party-roles.ts`). `pnpm check:ontology-bindings` fails the build when an IRI is not declared in the T-box or is the wrong kind: the code may not invent domain vocabulary. **A string-similarity heuristic standing in for a modelled relationship is the specific failure this prevents** — the Ask once looked for a treasury whose NAME resembled its owner's (`alice` → `alice.treasury`), which the ontology never claimed and the estate does not do, so paying "alice" dead-ended because hers is `alice2.treasury`. A planner prompt is the most tempting place to write a domain rule and the worst place to keep one: prompts say how to BEHAVE, the ontology says how the world is SHAPED, and prompt text should be rendered from the binding ([spec 355](specs/355-ontology-driven-ask-and-orchestration.md) + [agent-rules/ontology-drives-behavior.md](docs/architecture/agent-rules/ontology-drives-behavior.md)).
- **No third-party multi-sig.** We ship our own; port patterns from Safe (signature packing) but no runtime deps.
- **Per-package context budget:** `CLAUDE.md` ≤ 60 lines, `AUDIT.md` ≤ 150 lines, `README.md` ≤ 1800 words. If you're tempted to bloat one, fix the package shape.
- **No `eth_getLogs` in product read paths.** Package and app hot paths use `readContract` only; chain history and human-readable reverse strings come from on-chain storage, an explicit indexer, or app cache — never inline log scans ([ADR-0012](docs/architecture/decisions/0012-no-eth-getlogs-in-product-read-paths.md)). No exceptions: the last walker (`agent-naming` `reverseResolve` log fallback) was removed once spec/222 `reverseResolveString` landed.
- **No silent fallbacks.** A read/auth path has exactly ONE mechanism. If the canonical path has no answer it returns `null`/empty or throws — it does NOT escalate to a second, different, more expensive mechanism (`try fast catch slow`, empty-result → log walk, strong-check → weak-check). Empty is an answer, not a trigger. Bounded retries of the *same* call and cache-first reads (cache holds the canonical answer) are fine; switching mechanism is not ([ADR-0013](docs/architecture/decisions/0013-no-silent-fallbacks.md)).
- **The discovery knowledge base holds ONLY public, on-chain-derivable data.** The GraphDB A-box (projected by `demo-discovery-indexer`, read by `demo-discovery-mcp`/`-a2a`) is a world-readable read tier — populate it ONLY with facts any party could reproduce from on-chain state, transactions, or event logs (naming, registry entries, profile, relationships, attestations, custodian/passkey membership from `CustodianAdded`/`PasskeyAdded` logs). NEVER per-viewer state, session/credential secrets, off-chain/relying-app-local data, vault contents, PII, or private linkage ([ADR-0025](docs/architecture/decisions/0025-related-agents-are-private-vault-credentials.md)) — not even hashed, not even temporarily, not even to make a check faster. No secret-derived rows: any opaque form (e.g. custody membership as `sha256(credential‖smartAgent)`) MUST be a public, unsalted, on-chain-*reproducible* function — no server pepper. The indexer is the only writer; consumer services MUST NOT write viewer-/request-derived facts back. The claim: reading the whole KB reveals nothing reading the chain wouldn't ([ADR-0040](docs/architecture/decisions/0040-knowledge-base-only-public-onchain-data.md)). Amended carve-outs, each opt-in, signed or verified, and read the way a stranger would read it: A2A cards (286), signed Home manifests (323), and **public + owner-released shelf works** with their retrieval index (413; the index is never bound beside a vault, `check:no-vector-over-vault`).
- **On the A2A→MCP hop, Web3 is the authority; MCP OAuth is only the ingress envelope.** When an A2A service agent calls an MCP service, the authorization decision is ALWAYS the Web3 chain — on-chain delegation + signature validation (ERC-1271/6492/ECDSA/WebAuthn via `UniversalSignatureValidator`) + entitlement + tool policy (+ key-release grant + audit for data) — re-run server-side against the principal SA. We ship MCP's OAuth (RFC 9728 bearer / protected-resource metadata) as a generic ingress *adapter* so external MCP clients interoperate, but the bearer is at most a transport envelope: it carries only `ap_principal` + a `ap_grant_ref`/`ap_grant_hash` to an encrypted grant bundle, NEVER delegation/entitlement payload or field-level authority. Do NOT let OAuth drift inward: field/record access is never encoded in scopes (`vault:read` ≠ "decrypt all PII"), OAuth claims (`scope`/`aud`/`client_id`) are never trusted *as* the grant, an inbound MCP token is never reused for the next hop (fresh token / RFC 8693 exchange), and the Web3 gates are never skipped because "OAuth already authenticated the caller." For internal A2A→MCP hops the public OAuth ingress may be absent/skipped (the A2A bridge handles MCP tokens server-side, never exposing them downstream) — the delegation+signature+entitlement+policy gates are not. One mechanism (ADR-0013): OAuth-fail rejects, it does not fall back to a weaker token-trust path. When the standard and the substrate conflict, the substrate wins. ERC-4337 SA-bound, signature-verified, on-chain-revocable delegation is our differentiator from OAuth — never trade it for an opaque issuer-granted scope ([ADR-0041](docs/architecture/decisions/0041-web3-authority-not-oauth-on-a2a-to-mcp.md) + [spec 277](specs/277-mcp-delegated-vault-authorization.md) + [spec 269](specs/269-async-delegation-authorized-a2a.md) + [spec 270](specs/270-del-001-activation.md)).
- **Agentic architecture: first-party web expresses INTENTS to an A2A agent; it NEVER drives MCP directly.** A2A is the orchestration / intelligence / discovery layer — it discovers which capability satisfies a declarative goal, plans (planner/LLM/LangChain), assembles authority (delegation/entitlement), and composes MCP tools AS PRIMITIVES. A first-party UI (demo-web-pro, demo-sso-next) says "rebalance the treasury" / "read Alice's PII for purpose X" — a goal, not a tool name, payload, or call sequence — and posts it to an a2a agent. **`web → (edge) → MCP /tools|/mcp|/mcp/native` directly is the anti-pattern (RPC with extra steps) for first-party apps.** The public MCP ingress (`/mcp`, `/mcp/native`) is interop for EXTERNAL agents/clients only; the Act6 OAuth/Native panels are external-MCP-client *demonstrations of a primitive*, NOT a product-UX model (keep them labelled as such). **Orchestration is a Ring-0 primitive, not external** — the agentic loop (intent→plan→compose→observe→re-plan), the planner/reasoner PORT, and the tool/skill model live in a pure `packages/*` orchestration core; ONLY the concrete LLM-framework binding (LangChain/OpenAI/Anthropic SDK) lives in a dedicated in-repo adapter behind the port — the `chain-state` + `chain-state-viem` pattern (ADR-0037 exiles protocol-conformance + deployed-infra, NOT libraries). `service-agent` stays the deterministic compile/run target; the Ring-0 orchestration core PRODUCES its plans. Apps wire model creds/config; they don't re-implement the loop. The edge is orthogonal: it admits, it does not orchestrate (ADR-0043). Honest tension: the first-party web↔a2a surface is RPC-shaped TODAY (`/session/*`, `/account/*`, `/mcp/person/pii` — verbs); the intent layer (A2A messages/tasks against `A2aTaskDO` + `service-agent` plans) is the objective — build NEW first-party surfaces intent-first; the relayer verbs are legacy. Enforced by review + `check:no-direct-mcp-in-web` (browser-app source; allowlist the labelled external-client demo panels) ([ADR-0044](docs/architecture/decisions/0044-a2a-is-the-agentic-orchestration-layer.md) + [docs/architecture/principles.md](docs/architecture/principles.md)).
- **Packages are GENERIC trust building blocks; white-label / vertical / deployment code lives in apps.** `packages/*` are reusable + vertical-agnostic. They MUST NOT contain (a) **branding / vertical content** — faith vocabulary (ministry, congregation, discipleship, parish, denomination, gospel, scripture — **`church`/`circle` are the one carve-out: Ring-0 derived AGENT TYPES since 2026-08-30, ADR-0061 amendment; the KIND is generic, the content is not**), white-label/product names, marketing copy, themes; (b) **vertical-specific flows** — faith onboarding steps, impact-portal features; (c) **deployment specifics** — concrete hostnames (`impact-agent.me`/`.io`), the `demo.agent` subregistry, hosting providers (`*.pages.dev`/`*.workers.dev`/`vercel`), host/subdomain parsing. All of that is the **app** layer's job, supplied as an **app-level white-label config object** (spec 234) the generic core consumes. The `.agent` TLD itself is the naming protocol (owned by `agent-naming`) and is allowed. Enforced by `pnpm check:no-domain-in-packages` (hostnames + `demo.agent` + faith vocab) + `check:forbidden-terms`; the rest is doctrine ([ADR-0021](docs/architecture/decisions/0021-generic-packages-vs-white-label-apps.md)). Each app centralizes its domain/white-label literals in one module (e.g. `src/lib/domain.ts`), never scattered.

- **Many-registries hypothesis: be what registries are built FROM, not a tenant of one.** Hundreds of (mostly vertical) agent registries will exist; no horizontal winner. Ring 0 ships a generic **registry kit** — SA-anchored registry contracts with pluggable membership/validation hooks, discovery/registry SDK, signed agent-card + binding-proof standards — drawing concepts from ERC-8004/HCS as design inputs, never conformance targets. Specific registries (vertical or 8004/ANS/HCS bridges) are external consumers of the kit ([ADR-0038](docs/architecture/decisions/0038-many-registries-hypothesis-registry-building-primitives.md)).
- **This repo is Ring 0: primitives only. Integration + product-UX layers live in EXTERNAL repos.** Code that speaks someone else's protocol (ERC-8004 sync, ANS/DNS, HCS/UAID, OASF, Veramo) or runs as deployed infra (indexer, discovery API, explorer) belongs in sibling repos (`agentic-trust`, `agent-indexer`, `agent-explorer`, `oasf`) that import `@agenticprimitives/*` — never the reverse. Ring 0's job is expressive extension points (SA-signed cards, attestation schemas, complete events), not bridges. Learn from ERC-8004/HCS/sibling repos; port *primitive* patterns in, route *conformance* code out ([ADR-0037](docs/architecture/decisions/0037-primitives-pure-repo-external-integration-and-ux-layers.md)).
